=========================================================
C++ TEMPLATES - MUST DO INTERVIEW QUESTIONS & ANSWERS
=========================================================

1. What are Templates? ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
---------------------------------------------------------
Answer:
Templates are a compile-time feature that allows writing generic code.
They enable a function or class to work with different data types without
rewriting the same logic.

Example:
template<typename T>
T add(T a, T b)
{
    return a + b;
}

---------------------------------------------------------

2. Why do we need Templates? ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
---------------------------------------------------------
Answer:
Templates eliminate code duplication and improve code reusability by allowing
the same implementation to work with multiple data types.

Benefits:
✔ Generic programming
✔ Code reuse
✔ Type safety
✔ Compile-time polymorphism

---------------------------------------------------------

3. What is a Function Template? ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
---------------------------------------------------------
Answer:
A function template allows a single function to operate on different data
types. The compiler generates the appropriate function during compilation.

Example:
template<typename T>
T maxValue(T a, T b)
{
    return (a > b) ? a : b;
}

---------------------------------------------------------

4. What is a Class Template? ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
---------------------------------------------------------
Answer:
A class template allows creating generic classes that can work with different
data types.

Example:
template<typename T>
class Stack
{
    T data[100];
};

---------------------------------------------------------

5. What is Template Instantiation? ⭐⭐⭐⭐
---------------------------------------------------------
Answer:
Template instantiation is the process where the compiler generates a concrete
function or class from a template when it is used with a specific type.

Example:
maxValue<int>(10, 20);
maxValue<double>(3.5, 8.2);

---------------------------------------------------------

6. What is Template Type Deduction? ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
---------------------------------------------------------
Answer:
The compiler automatically deduces the template type from the function
arguments.

Example:
maxValue(10,20);       // T = int
maxValue(2.5,3.8);     // T = double

---------------------------------------------------------

7. What is Template Specialization? ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
---------------------------------------------------------
Answer:
Template specialization allows providing a custom implementation for a
specific type when the generic implementation is not suitable.

Example:
template<>
void print<bool>(bool b)
{
    cout << "Boolean: " << b;
}

---------------------------------------------------------

8. What is Partial Template Specialization? ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
---------------------------------------------------------
Partial Template Specialization is the process of providing a specialized implementation of a class template
for a category of types, while leaving the remaining template parameters generic.

Why is it called "Partial" Specialization?
Because you are not specializing one exact type.
You're saying:
"Whenever the type is a pointer, use this version."
You're specializing a category of types, not a single type.
Answer:
Partial specialization customizes a class template for a group of types
instead of one exact type.

Step 1: Generic Template
This works for every type.

template<typename T>
class Test
{
public:
    void display()
    {
        cout << "Generic";
    }
};

Now create objects:

Test<int> t1;
Test<double> t2;
Test<string> t3;

Step 2: Suppose you want all pointer types to behave differently

Instead of writing:

Test<int*>
Test<double*>
Test<char*>
Test<float*>

one by one, you write one specialization that matches any pointer.

template<typename T>
class Test<T*>
{
public:
    void display()
    {
        cout << "Pointer Version";
    }
};



Note:
Function templates do NOT support partial specialization.

---------------------------------------------------------

9. Why don't Function Templates support Partial Specialization? ⭐⭐⭐⭐
---------------------------------------------------------
Answer:
Function overloading already provides similar functionality, making partial
specialization unnecessary and potentially ambiguous.

---------------------------------------------------------

10. What is a Non-Type Template Parameter? ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
---------------------------------------------------------
Answer:
A non-type template parameter accepts a compile-time constant value instead
of a type.

Example:
template<typename T, int SIZE>
class Array
{
    T arr[SIZE];
};

---------------------------------------------------------

11. Difference between 'typename' and 'class'? ⭐⭐⭐⭐
---------------------------------------------------------
Answer:
In template parameter lists, both 'typename' and 'class' are equivalent.

Example:
template<typename T>
template<class T>

Both are valid.

Use 'typename' when referring to nested dependent types.

---------------------------------------------------------

12. What are Variadic Templates? ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
---------------------------------------------------------
Answer:
Variadic templates allow functions or classes to accept any number of
template arguments.

Example:
template<typename... Args>
void print(Args... args);

Introduced in C++11.

---------------------------------------------------------

13. What is Perfect Forwarding? ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
---------------------------------------------------------
Answer:
Perfect forwarding preserves the value category (lvalue/rvalue) of arguments
when forwarding them to another function.

It is implemented using:
✔ Universal reference (T&&)
✔ std::forward

---------------------------------------------------------

14. Difference between std::move and std::forward? ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
---------------------------------------------------------
Answer:

std::move
---------
Always converts an object to an rvalue.

std::forward
------------
Conditionally preserves the original value category and is mainly used in
templates for perfect forwarding.

---------------------------------------------------------

15. What is SFINAE? ⭐⭐⭐⭐
---------------------------------------------------------
Answer:
SFINAE stands for "Substitution Failure Is Not An Error".

If substituting template arguments makes a template invalid, the compiler
removes that template from overload resolution instead of producing an error.

---------------------------------------------------------

16. What is if constexpr? ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
---------------------------------------------------------
Answer:
Introduced in C++17.

It performs compile-time conditional branching. The branch whose condition is
false is discarded during compilation.

Example:
if constexpr(std::is_integral_v<T>)
{
    // Integral-specific code
}

---------------------------------------------------------

17. What are Type Traits? ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
---------------------------------------------------------
Answer:
Type traits are compile-time utilities that provide information about types
or transform types.

Examples:
std::is_same
std::is_integral
std::is_pointer
std::remove_reference

---------------------------------------------------------

18. What is Compile-time Polymorphism? ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
---------------------------------------------------------
Answer:
Compile-time polymorphism is achieved when the compiler resolves function or
class behavior during compilation.

Examples:
✔ Function overloading
✔ Operator overloading
✔ Templates

---------------------------------------------------------

19. What are the advantages of Templates? ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
---------------------------------------------------------
Answer:
✔ Code reuse
✔ Type safety
✔ Better maintainability
✔ Compile-time polymorphism
✔ No runtime overhead
✔ Generic programming

---------------------------------------------------------

20. What are the disadvantages of Templates? ⭐⭐⭐⭐
---------------------------------------------------------
Answer:
✘ Longer compilation time
✘ Code bloat due to multiple instantiations
✘ Complex compiler error messages
✘ Increased binary size

